{"title":"Old Masters, New Media","link":"https://kottke.org/25/10/old-masters-new-media-1","date":1759502420000,"content":"\n        <p>In a five part series called “emoji-nation”, Ukrainian Nastya Ptichek mixes the work of well-known painters with graphical elements of new media. In <a href=\"https://www.behance.net/gallery/emoji-nation-part-2/15413671\">the second part</a> of the series, the works of Edward Hopper are augmented with social media interface icons:</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"/cdn-cgi/image/format=auto,fit=scale-down,width=1200,metadata=none/plus/misc/images/nastya-ptichek-01.jpg\" srcset=\"/cdn-cgi/image/format=auto,fit=scale-down,width=500,metadata=none/plus/misc/images/nastya-ptichek-01.jpg 500w, /cdn-cgi/image/format=auto,fit=scale-down,width=1200,metadata=none/plus/misc/images/nastya-ptichek-01.jpg 1200w\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"640\" height=\"400\" alt=\"Nastya Ptichek\" /></p>\n\n<p>The <a href=\"https://www.behance.net/gallery/emoji-nation/15294433\">first part</a> finds emoji doppelgangers for works of fine art while the <a href=\"https://www.behance.net/gallery/emoji-nation-part-3/15443107\">third part</a> uses paintings as movie poster imagery for the likes of Kill Bill and Home Alone (paired with Munch’s The Scream). For <a href=\"https://www.behance.net/gallery/emoji-nation-part-4/15470267\">part four</a>, Ptichek places modal dialogs over art works:</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"/cdn-cgi/image/format=auto,fit=scale-down,width=1200,metadata=none/plus/misc/images/nastya-ptichek-02.jpg\" srcset=\"/cdn-cgi/image/format=auto,fit=scale-down,width=500,metadata=none/plus/misc/images/nastya-ptichek-02.jpg 500w, /cdn-cgi/image/format=auto,fit=scale-down,width=1200,metadata=none/plus/misc/images/nastya-ptichek-02.jpg 1200w\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"640\" height=\"866\" alt=\"Nastya Ptichek\" /></p>\n\n<p>And <a href=\"https://www.behance.net/gallery/emoji-nation-part-5/15853319\">part five</a> plays around with several Google interface elements:</p>\n\n<p><img src=\"/cdn-cgi/image/format=auto,fit=scale-down,width=1200,metadata=none/plus/misc/images/nastya-ptichek-03.jpg\" srcset=\"/cdn-cgi/image/format=auto,fit=scale-down,width=500,metadata=none/plus/misc/images/nastya-ptichek-03.jpg 500w, /cdn-cgi/image/format=auto,fit=scale-down,width=1200,metadata=none/plus/misc/images/nastya-ptichek-03.jpg 1200w\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"640\" height=\"446\" alt=\"Nastya Ptichek\" /></p>\n\n<p>Love this kind of thing. Feels like I’ve seen something like it before though. Anyone recall?</p>\n\n         <p><em>[This is a vintage post originally from Apr 2014.]</em></p>\n\n         <p><strong>Tags:</strong> <a href=\"https://kottke.org/tag/art\">art</a> · <a href=\"https://kottke.org/tag/Nastya%20Ptichek\">Nastya Ptichek</a> · <a href=\"https://kottke.org/tag/remix\">remix</a> · <a href=\"https://kottke.org/tag/timeless%20posts\">timeless posts</a></p>\n\n        \n\n    ","author":"Jason Kottke","siteTitle":"Kottke","siteHash":"89a883271b4598f9b33ebe87be70d38557bcd266e68aee521a8f93622e0ab723","entryHash":"74b4d41b30c870110db8ada43334be53554af34b0e65be2adb4d900a539902b7","category":"Sites"}